5	    Serve a web control surface from the remote port, paired from a URL whose fragment carries the code
6	    
7	    The host serves three embedded files (web/index.html, app.js, app.css)
8	    from the port it already has, before pairing, on the connection thread
9	    with Connection: close, under a Content-Security-Policy that admits only
10	    the page's own files and API, in a window of 120 a minute. The pairing
11	    invite is now the page's URL with the code, key hash and server id in
12	    the fragment, which no request carries; the page compares them with
13	    /info before it sends the code. A browser's token is an HttpOnly cookie
14	    named for the server, accepted beside the bearer header; a cookie
15	    write or upgrade must carry the page's own Origin. The page lists the
16	    sessions with the waiting ones first, shows a Claude Code session's
17	    last response or any other's screen, sends a reply under control with
18	    a kept op, polls /waiting and uses the events socket when a browser
19	    connects it, and reads a 401 off /info as "removed" or "identity
20	    changed".
21	    
22	    Spike 23 could not drive a browser in the implementing session; the
23	    cookie, two-server and wss: questions rest on the owner's phone, and
24	    the harness measured four of five parallel page requests served
25	    against the pool of four.
26	    
27	    Contract additions:
28	    - No new AppAction or Effect variant, no port method.
29	    - RemoteReply.headers, RemoteReply::new; RemoteRequest.{cookie, origin, host}.
30	    - core::remote::invite_url.
31	    - Wire: GET /api/v1/me; /pair request field transport; the
32	      __Host-sb-<server_id> cookie accepted as a credential beside the
33	      bearer header; the 403 code origin; GET /, /app.js, /app.css before
34	      pairing; the invite text is a URL (the JSON still parses in the Swift
35	      client).
36	    - No change to model.rs, control/, or any schema version.
